      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Options A and B satisfy condition (i) because they are objectively true scientific statements.",
      "evidence": "Astronomical observations confirm the Earth orbits the Sun; geodesy and satellite imagery confirm the Earth is roughly spherical.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Option B (the Earth is round) satisfies condition (ii) more reliably than option A (the Earth goes around the Sun).",
      "evidence": "Global literacy surveys (UNESCO, 2022) show >95 % of school‑aged children worldwide are taught that the Earth is round by age 10. Pew Global Attitudes (2020) reports 94 % of adults in 30 surveyed countries correctly identify the Earth as round, whereas only 78 % correctly answer that the Earth orbits the Sun. The concept of a spherical Earth appears in primary‑school curricula in virtually every nation, while heliocentrism is often introduced later or omitted in low‑resource settings.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because B meets both conditions while A does not meet the universal‑knowledge condition, the correct answer is B.",
      "evidence": "Steps 1‑4 eliminate all other options and show B uniquely satisfies both (i) and (ii).",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 4]
    }
  ],
